# =============================================================================
# numerus — Retired numeric marker hole (expect parse error).
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • The marker holes `numerus<_>` and `fractus<_>` (en `int<_>` / `float<_>`) retired with the wrapped numeric spelling; there is no wrapped form left to leave a hole in
#   • Parse error — `PARSE040:numeric_wrapper_retired` names the form written; bare `numerus` is the default `i64`, and a bare marker such as `i32` is the sized type
#   • The policy words keep their hole (`modulus<_>`, `saturatus<_>`)
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • writing `numerus<_>` to let the width be inferred — name the marker (`i32`) or use bare `numerus`
#
# See also: numerus, fractus
# =============================================================================

# retired numeric marker hole (expect parse error)
#
# WHY: a width marker is its own type; the wrapped form, holes included, is retired.
#
# DELIBERATE: this exemplum keeps the retired spelling (`int<_>`) on purpose, to
# pin the PARSE040 `numeric_wrapper_retired` diagnostic for the hole form (N7d).

main {
    let int<_> width ← 1 ∷ i32
    print width
}
